            "text": "david fincher’s \"the social network,\" released in 2010, stands as one of the defining cinematic achievements of the twenty-first century. written by aaron sorkin and based on ben mezrich’s book \"the accidental billionaires,\" the film is less a literal documentary about the founding of facebook and more a modern shakespearean tragedy about ambition, class, friendship, and the bitter irony of communication in the digital era.\n\nat the heart of the film is mark zuckerberg, played with chilling brilliance by jesse eisenberg. eisenberg portrays zuckerberg as a hyper-intelligent, socially alienated coder whose rapid-fire intellect is matched only by his emotional detachment. the opening scene, a breakneck conversation in a noisy harvard bar between mark and his girlfriend erica albright (rooney mara), sets the entire narrative engine into motion. within minutes, sorkin’s razor-sharp dialogue establishes mark’s crippling insecurity, his obsession with elite social clubs, and his tendency to burn bridges before they are even built. when erica dumps him, the bruised ego of a gifted programmer directly sparks the creation of facemash, the precursor to facebook.\n\nthe film’s narrative structure is masterfully handled through non-linear storytelling. fincher cuts between the early days of harvard dorm-room coding, wild nights in california, and two contentious legal depositions years later. on one side of the legal battle are the winklevoss twins (both played with stiff, entitled grace by armie hammer) and divya narendra, who claim mark stole their idea for the harvard connection. on the other side is eduardo saverin, mark’s only real friend and original chief financial officer, played by andrew garfield in a career-best, deeply empathetic performance.\n\ngarfield serves as the emotional anchor of the film. while mark views the world through cold algorithms and limitless expansion, eduardo approaches the project with conventional loyalty and business 