            "text": "**Pedophilia** is defined in psychology and medicine as a psychiatric condition in which an adult or older adolescent experiences a persistent, intense, and primary or exclusive sexual attraction to prepubescent children (typically aged 11 or younger). \n\nHere are key aspects of how it is understood clinically and legally:\n\n### 1. Clinical Definition\nIn diagnostic manuals, such as the American Psychiatric Association’s *Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders* (DSM-5) and the World Health Organization’s *International Classification of Diseases* (ICD-11):\n* **Pedophilia (as a Paraphilia):** Refers to the pattern of sexual attraction itself. \n* **Pedophilic Disorder:** Diagnosed when an individual acts on these urges, or when the urges cause marked distress or interpersonal difficulty for the individual. The diagnostic criteria typically specify that the individual must be at least 16 years old and at least five years older than the child to whom they are attracted.\n\n### 2. Distinction Between Attraction and Action\nMental health professionals make a critical distinction between:\n* **The Attraction (Pedophilia):** An internal psychological condition or sexual preference.\n* **The Act (Child Sexual Abuse):** A severe crime involving sexual acts with a minor. \n\nNot all people who experience pedophilic attraction act on it; many seek psychological or medical help to manage or suppress these feelings. Conversely, some individuals who commit child sexual abuse do not meet the clinical criteria for pedophilia; their offenses may instead be driven by opportunism, antisocial traits, power, or alcohol and drug disinhibition.\n\n### 3. Treatment and Prevention\nPedophilia is considered an enduring pattern of attraction that cannot simply be \"cured,\" but it can be managed. Preventative interventions and clinical treatments focus entirely on ensuring that an individual **never harms a child**. Common approaches include:\n* **Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT):** Helping individuals identify triggers, manage impulses, and develop healthy coping mechanisms.\n* **Pharmacological Treatments:** In some severe cases, medications (such as anti-androgens or SSRIs) may be used under strict medical supervision to lower libido and reduce compulsive sexual thoughts.\n* **Confidential Help Lines:** Several countries operate confidential, non-judgmental prevention initiatives (such as *Stop It Now!* or Germany’s *Kein Täter werden*) aimed at encouraging people with these attractions to seek help before any offense occurs.\n\n### 4. Legal Status\nWhile having thoughts or attractions is a psychiatric issue, **any sexual contact with a child, or the creation, possession, or distribution of child sexual abuse material (CSAM), is universally recognized as a severe crime** carrying harsh criminal penalties under national and international law.",
